00:00:00.369 --> 00:00:02.720
Film Courage: How many Thomas&nbsp;
Caine books have you written?

00:00:02.720 --> 00:00:08.640
Andrew Warren, Author: Three novels and four&nbsp;
novellas. Two of those novellas I co-wrote&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:13.840
actually with another author and there's a&nbsp;
fourth book that has not been published yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.160
that's with an agent who I think&nbsp;
you asked about this before but he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:23.760
considering shopping it around we're&nbsp;
considering maybe signing with a publisher&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:27.280
and seeing what that might mean and&nbsp;
what might be out there in that avenue.

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:28.240
Film Courage: So what is that?

00:00:28.240 --> 00:00:30.480
Andrew: It's about eight Thomas Caine books.

00:00:30.480 --> 00:00:33.280
Film Courage: And why haven't&nbsp;
you tired of this character yet?

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:38.480
Andrew: I don't know I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:46.080
part of it is because I did keep his past&nbsp;
mysterious so there's always something else to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:51.680
reveal I think if I had one of the things I wanted&nbsp;
to avoid was that I saw in a lot of thrillers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:56.400
I call the resume where they'll be like oh we're&nbsp;
going bring in this guy, these ex-special forces&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.120
and he served five years in Libya and they kind&nbsp;
of go down as the whole thing and to me that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:07.280
kind of takes the mysticism out takes something&nbsp;
away from it because I sort of feel like well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:11.920
if the only thing that's special about him is&nbsp;
that he's a Navy Seal who did these things that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:16.560
any Navy Seal did those things is the same&nbsp;
it's sort of so I wanted to kind of avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:23.600
filling in like every little detail and and by&nbsp;
doing that I think it allows me to kind of find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:29.760
new angles and new things that I find interesting&nbsp;
about him because there is a big black space in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.760 --> 00:01:35.120
the background so his his background is is a&nbsp;
mystery all we know is that he was betrayed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:41.280
at a certain point in his life but is he a&nbsp;
mystery to himself or you don't go that far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.280 --> 00:01:47.760
into his thought well I try to go a little bit&nbsp;
into his thoughts but not too much because I feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:52.560
like in fact I originally when I first conceived&nbsp;
the series one thing I did change was I originally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:57.600
was going to write at first person where you would&nbsp;
hear his thoughts and I realized I was like well&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.160 --> 00:02:02.000
if I do that then I feel like i'm giving too&nbsp;
much away because you're always going to know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:07.440
what he's thinking so a lot of time so I switched&nbsp;
it to third person and a lot of times when someone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:12.560
asks him a direct really probing question I'll&nbsp;
have him just like look at the person and not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:18.320
answer and I try to let the reader kind of&nbsp;
project what they think the answer might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.320 --> 00:02:27.680
onto it so I really do make a concerted effort to&nbsp;
as much without sacrificing character and making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:32.720
him someone believable and someone you can invest&nbsp;
yourself in I’d make a concerted effort to keep as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:39.040
much of him in the dark as possible so that&nbsp;
because I think that allows the reader to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:42.880
inject a little of themselves and their&nbsp;
opinions and their beliefs into his character&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:51.040
why is he doing this sort of like as an example&nbsp;
in that first the first book Tokyo black he's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:58.240
he is sort of driven to help this girl and he's&nbsp;
it's never really explained why he sees a picture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.240 --> 00:03:03.840
